[{:db/id -1, :doc/id "doc-ayatori-bench-coscientist-iteration-14", :doc/title "ayatori Co-Scientist Iteration 14 — the served number was the machine paging; the judge now refuses a swapped-out SUT", :doc/path "bench/coscientist-iteration-14.edn", :doc/body "# Co-Scientist iteration 14 - the served number was the machine paging, not the engine; the judge now refuses a swapped-out SUT\n\n> Seed: iteration 13's S1 - IC08 served at 236 ms p50 against 0.77 ms in\n> the in-JVM harness (2026-08-14), three suspects. Judge: bench/benchmark.edn\n> v2 (LDBC SNB Interactive over the network).\n\n## Generate (predictions, written before running)\n\n  H-person       IC08 with highest-degree persons >= 10x below official\n                 persons on the same subset (the seed's own guess)\n  H-concurrency  official persons at inflight 1 within 1.5x of iteration\n                 13's inflight-8 236 ms (concurrency is not the 300x)\n  H-scan         a 10x smaller subset cuts IC08 by ~10x (a scan)\n\nArms, all origin :loopback, rate 1 for 24 s, SUT 8 threads:\n\n  A  official persons,      35,000-post subset, inflight 1\n  B  highest-degree persons, 35,000-post subset, inflight 1\n  D  official persons,       3,500-post subset, inflight 1\n  E  highest-degree persons,  3,500-post subset, inflight 1\n\n## Rank - what the driver returned (p50 ms; server-ms in parentheses)\n\n    arm   ic02          ic07         ic08         ic09           empty  rows\n    A     361 (349)     85 (82)      310 (308)    1515 (1499)    12/24  7.0\n    B    1064 (1028)   141 (130)     163 (158)   20019 (19765)    0/24  19.2\n    D      47 (7)       16 (6)       224 (220)     988 (982)     20/24  3.3\n    E     186 (175)     16 (7)       142 (96)     1959 (1902)     5/24  12.8\n\n  in-process control, same var, same 3,500-post subset, fresh JVM:\n    highest-degree  rows 4  first 30.4 ms  p50-of-30 1.90  min 1.15\n    official        rows 0  first 29.0 ms  p50-of-30 17.45 min 11.45\n    fresh thread    first 3.1  second 1.73\n\n## Reflect\n\n  - H-person REFUTED: B/A on IC08 is 1.9x, not 10x. H-scan REFUTED: a 10x\n    smaller subset moved IC08 1.4x. H-concurrency held (310 vs 236, 1.3x)\n    and so is not the cause either.\n  - server-ms equals client-ms everywhere: the time is inside the query\n    on the server. And the same var on the same data is 1.9 ms in a fresh\n    JVM. What differs is the JVM's residency: the served SUTs had RSS\n    0.02-0.03 GB against 0.9 GB of used heap (jcmd GC.heap_info), on a\n    host with 34.5 of 35.8 GB swap used, 0.2-0.4 GB free, 5.9M pages in\n    the compressor - and my own 20 GB full-SF1 JVM was part of it. Every\n    query paged its working set back in. Load average (12-25 beside every\n    row) did not show this; swap did.\n  - So iteration 13's 7.76 ops/s and its per-op p50s are numbers about\n    paging. The row stands, relabelled in this iteration's scoreboard note\n    as a baseline under paging, not rewritten (append-only).\n  - Seen on the way, not chased: in-process, the EMPTY IC08 answer (an\n    official person with no kept message) is 9x SLOWER than the 4-row\n    answer (17 vs 1.9 ms). The planner pays more to find nothing. That is\n    a real seed for the planner, filed below.\n\n## Evolve - what changed\n\n  - `bench/ldbc_snb_driver.cljk`: `host-memory` (free, swap total/used/%)\n    is written into every receipt beside the load average, and the run\n    REFUSES (exit 2) when swap-used exceeds --max-swap-pct (default 90).\n    Shown both ways: 97.8% > 50 refused; --max-swap-pct 100 ran (arm E2:\n    IC08 90 ms served, still 47x the in-process control, at 83% swap).\n  - `bench/benchmark.edn`: validity gains :sut-resident; growth's\n    \"written beside every row\" gains :host-memory. A judge that cannot\n    see the SUT being paged out scores the host.\n  - kotobase-peer bf72ade7: the driver change and the mechanism receipt\n    bench/results/2026-09-11-ldbc-snb-network-iteration-14-paged-out-sut.edn\n\n## Meta\n\nVerdict under bench/benchmark.edn v2: REFUSED-HOST. No throughput was\nvalidly measured; the iteration's product is a refusal the judge did not\nhave and the mechanism behind the previous row.\n\nThe loop's value this iteration: the seed named three plausible engine\ncauses, the arms refuted all three, and the control that settled it was\nthe cheapest one (the same function in a fresh process). Without the\ncontrol the next iteration would have been a planner change measured\nthrough paging.\n\nSeeds for iteration 15, in order:\n\n  - S0 (condition): a valid row needs a resident SUT. Either this host\n    below the swap threshold, or origin :cross-host on a fleet mac-mini\n    (the driver is the same; the origin changes). Nothing else is scored\n    until one of these holds.\n  - S1' (planner): the empty answer costs 9x the non-empty one on IC08.\n    Predicted: the plan starts from the unbound clause when the bound\n    person has no hasCreator datom; a cardinality check on the bound\n    clause first would make the empty case the cheapest.\n  - S2 (rung): full SF1 with IC09's two-hop join bounded (the warm-up\n    on one highest-degree person took minutes; B's IC09 on 35k posts was\n    20 s served). The date filter under the join, or friend-of-friend\n    bounded first.\n  - S3 (mix): short reads IS1-IS7.\n"}]
